@prefix hanoi: <http://example.org/orexis/hanoi#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/hanoi#> .
@prefix orexis: <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<http://example.org/orexis/hanoi> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Orexis hanoi" ;
    rdfs:comment """Tower of Hanoi, as a domain plug-in (#257). The claim under test is AGENTS.md's own — the domain is a plug-in, plant/water language is the example — and this package is the proof at its strongest: a classical planning domain that ships an ontology and six ground actions and NOT ONE LINE of Python. The goal is a world-ratified desire met by absence (a disk off the target peg); legality lives in each move's precondition; the state is graph triples an effect really retracts — the first retraction that deletes rather than upserts; and the optimal solution is nobody's algorithm, it is the cheapest achiever under orexis:costs.""" .

#################  The board  #################
#  THE PEGS ARE T-BOX INDIVIDUALS, and that placement is rule 1 doing its work: the six
#  ground actions must name the pegs, code (a package's query is code) may never name a
#  WORLD instance, and every Tower of Hanoi that has ever been posed has exactly these three
#  pegs — they are the domain's fixtures, exactly as orexis:Below is a fixture of violation.
#  The DISKS are the world's: a two-disk world and a three-disk world differ only there.

:Peg a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Peg" ;
    rdfs:comment "One of the three rods. A T-Box individual each, because the actions are ground per (from, to) pair and code may name only T-Box terms." .
:PegA a :Peg ; rdfs:label "peg A" ; rdfs:comment "Where every puzzle starts." .
:PegB a :Peg ; rdfs:label "peg B" ; rdfs:comment "The spare." .
:PegC a :Peg ; rdfs:label "peg C" ; rdfs:comment "Where every goal points." .

:Disk a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Disk" ;
    rdfs:comment "A world's individuals, sized. How many there are is the world's whole authorship of an instance of this puzzle." .

:size a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Disk ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "size" ;
    rdfs:comment "Smaller number, smaller disk. Legality is one comparison: a move onto a peg holding any smaller disk is no move at all — with an ordered start that is exactly the classical rule, said without naming tops." .

:on a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain :Disk ;
    rdfs:label "on" ;
    rdfs:comment "What a disk rests on — another disk, or a peg. RUNTIME STATE, never authored in a world: it lives in the state graph, effects retract and add it, and the chain (hanoi:on)+ down to a peg is what 'on peg P' means everywhere in this package's queries." .
